# The Dark Ages: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8], "dark-ages", "The Dark Ages",
      "Six players · in progress",
      "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants, and the Warlord. "
      "Four good years before the population comes, and a crown in a hurry to build.",
      short="6 players")
REVOLT_LINE = True   # briefs.py puts the causes for revolt on every board card (the Director's choice for this game)

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Scholar, so the Emperor appoints someone to read I Ching and count the population, and pays the appointee whatever she chooses (Note 3). Each realia province holds two pieces of its own realia, and each collector needs one from each of the other three. The Warlord starts with two soldiers and may hire one more, for 20, after any march (Section 14).")
act("Six booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each take two pieces of their own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built. Nobody sits in Provinces 4 or 7, so nothing is owed there.", map_hl=[2,3])
# Reina's first prompt: who reads I Ching (then the shuffle). Rob's first prompt: his two soldiers (place_army) and his tax stance.
# ===== CHAPTER 1: I Can Count to Three
place_army({"W1":"H8", "W2":"O18"}, "Robert Land stands his two soldiers in his own desert: W1 on H8, against Rich's Province 3, and W2 on O18, the finger of desert that touches Silke's Province 5. He declares he won't pay the tax on his own harvest; the Warlord may refuse.")
say("Rob", "Evening, all. Two soldiers, two nice quiet spots in my own desert. Nothing to worry about, Rich, Silke, they're just enjoying the view. As for tax, Reina, Your Majesty, the desert doesn't pay tax. The desert *is* the tax. Anyone who wants my soldiers off the board knows the price: twenty each, and I'll smile while I take it.",
 "Rob: One soldier is a single step from Rich's fields and the other a single step from Silke's coast, and from there Clark and Paddy are only a short walk away. I win by raiding all four of them, so let them all watch the throne while I take a little from everyone.")
say("Reina", "The heavens speak to the throne, so the throne will read them. I'll shuffle the twenty cards myself and take nothing for it; the omens aren't for sale at this table. And Rob, enjoy the view while you can. The Wall was built to watch exactly that kind of desert.",
 "Reina: If I'm the one turning the cards, nobody can slip a bad omen in as an excuse to call a revolt, and it costs the treasury nothing. Rob's twenty-a-head price is noted, but I don't pay ransom.")
act("Reina appoints herself to the empty Scholar's job: she'll read an I Ching card each year from Year 2 and keep the population counter, unpaid. She shuffles the twenty cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[1])

# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, what a pleasure to farm under such a steady hand, and under such a fine Wall. Silke, Paddy, the customary five each, if you'd be so kind. It's the old rate, the honest rate, and I won't ask a grain more of you this year. You have my word, and my word is the one thing I don't charge for. Rob, do enjoy the view. Just remember my granary feeds the whole table, your soldiers included.",
 "Rich: Five is the floor, and in Year One the floor buys me goodwill. I want both peasants thinking of me as the kind landlord, so that when the crown wobbles they'll remember who was kind to them. I'm also keeping an eye on Rob's man at H8, sitting right on my doorstep.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, while the fields are full. A dynasty is built in the good years, not begged for in the lean ones. The Summer Palace goes up first; the Wall and the Canal come after it. Clark, five for the collecting, by the book. Rob, the desert can call itself whatever it likes, but your share is twenty-four, and I'll be counting it.",
 "Reina: The Summer Palace is cheap, and it unlocks the Wall links and my own Canal, so I'm getting the gate open in Year 1. Forty brings in 96 even if Rob won't pay, which leaves me about 131 in the reserve to start the heavy building next year.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 40%: 24 from each of Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy, 96 in all. Rob refused the tax at the setup, and the Warlord may. The crown builds the Summer Palace alone, for 20, and pays Clark his 5 as the Official at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.40, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Twenty-four from each of us, Clark included, and I'll count it out in front of everyone. Rob, your refusal goes in the ledger: Year One, Warlord declines the crown's due. Your Majesty, forty percent in the first year is bold. I'll write that down too, purely as a record of the good years, you understand.",
 "Clark: Forty percent and a Warlord who already pays nothing. That's two causes for revolt starting to form, and I've noted the year and the rate for each. A throne the table hands you is a throne your friends hold up, so for now I'm the most honest man in the room.")
say("Reina", "The Summer Palace stands at G20, in the heart of the province, under the Wall and well clear of the dunes. Let the desert admire it from a distance, Rob. A respectful distance.",
 "Reina: G20 is on the list of squares no Warlord soldier can reach this year or next, so Rob can't send a raid there for the 20-grain toll any time soon. A palace is only a monument if it's still standing.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G20", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G20, in the middle of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 870 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: each seller puts up one piece from stock and sets its minimum: Clark's Customs House, Rich's Land Deed Scroll, Silke's Dragon Jar, Paddy's Wooden Bowl. Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House goes up at two, honest price, and anyone who wants the crown's paperwork on their mantel is welcome to it. I'll bid modestly on the rest. I won't be the man who poured the table's grain into the Harvest Pile in Year One, and I'll remember who does.",
 "Clark: Thirty-six grain and I need three pieces plus twenty in hand, so I'm buying cheap or not at all. If Rich or Silke start a bidding war, that goes in the ledger as well.")
say("Rich", "A magnificent Palace, Your Majesty. I'll be able to see it from my window, which is where I'll be counting what's left after forty percent. Friends, my Land Deed opens at a modest three. I'm not one to gouge a neighbour in the first year. As for the rest of you, I'll bid like a gentleman: once, sensibly, and with no tantrums.",
 "Rich: Three quarters of every price goes back into my own Harvest Pile, so overpaying today only feeds the granary I run. Still, I'm keeping these bids sensible and every lot under ten, so I hold onto enough to rent friends with later. The Emperor just showed she'll take forty percent off us, and I'll remember that when people start counting their friends.")
say("Silke", "Let's count, shall we? Five to Rich, twenty-four to the crown, and I'm down to thirty-one from sixty. Paddy's the same. Clark has thirty-six, Rich forty-six, Rob sixty for doing nothing at all, and Her Majesty a hundred and thirty-six. So I'll bid small and honest, and anyone who wants a war over a Customs House can pay three-quarters of it into Rich's pile. My Dragon Jar starts at four. It's good porcelain, and the Palace will want something to put on its shelves.",
 "Silke: Forty percent in year one, for a palace I'll never sleep in. I'm keeping these bids cheap so I still have grain left, and I'm keeping count, because three of us paying twenty-four is the start of 'taxes are consistently excessive.'")
act("The Harvest Pile is the common bank: Rich keeps it and pays the harvests out of it, but its grain isn't his.", map_hl=[3])
say("Paddy", "Five for Rich, twenty-four for the crown, and the rice still in the paddies. I'll put in an honest bid or two, but I'm not starting a war over a jar in the first year. If someone else wants a piece more than I do, say so now and I'll stand aside and remember the favour. The Bowl goes up at two, same as anyone's.",
 "Paddy: Thirty-one grain and three pieces to find, so I'm only buying what comes cheap and leaving the rest. Whoever lets me have one at the floor price, I'll owe them, and I'll pay it back.")
say("Rob", "Bid away, friends, bid away. I don't need a single jar or bowl. Buy them, set them out nicely on your fields, and polish them up. And Your Majesty, a respectful distance is eight rounds. I've counted.",
 "Rob: Every piece they buy goes onto their own province, and my objective is to raid one piece from each of them, so I want them shopping. I keep my sixty grain dry for soldiers and tolls while they fight over the shelves.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed Scroll 3, Dragon Jar 4, Wooden Bowl 2. Rob passes on every lot. No lot is close enough at the top for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
DC = "Take it, then. I've noted what you paid."
DR = "Take it with my blessing. I'll get the next one cheaper."
DS = "Take it, then. I'd rather keep my grain than buy a trinket at war prices."
DP = "Go on then, it's yours. Mind you look after it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(6,5), B(6,None,DP), B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(3,7)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Paddy stopped at 5, Silke at 6; Rich's limit was 8. Rich puts the Customs House on L12.", at="L12")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(6,5), B(6,None,DP), B(2,6)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Paddy stopped at 5; Silke's limit and Clark's were both 6, and the tie goes to Clark's seat, so Silke bid no higher than 5. Clark puts the Land Deed Scroll on G14.", at="G14")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(2,6), B(2,None,DC), B(3,7)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 4. Paddy stopped at 4, Clark at 6; Rich's limit was 7. Rich puts the Dragon Jar on L13.", at="L13")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(2,6), B(2,None,DC), B(3,7)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 5, Clark at 6; Rich's limit was 7. Rich puts the Wooden Bowl on M12.", at="M12")
act("Rich holds a Customs House, a Dragon Jar and a Wooden Bowl, the three pieces his set needs, and 26.5 grain, over the 20 a set requires: a complete set. A player with a complete set may change places with another at any moment (Section 11), and the Gentry may take any seat, the throne included. He holds it for now.", map_hl=[3], big=True)
say("Rich", "Look at that, all three pieces on my fields in the first year. Purely ornamental, Your Majesty, purely ornamental. I've set them well back from the dunes, Rob, at a respectful distance. Eight rounds, I believe you said? And Silke, I'll take that back: the pile is the table's, not mine. I only count it.",
 "Rich: Customs House, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl: that's the whole set, and I'm sitting on enough grain to cover the twenty. I've tucked them deep in the south of my province, out of every soldier's reach, and I'll keep smiling at the Emperor until the day it pays better to stop.")
say("Clark", "The Land Deed goes on G14, well back from the dunes and in plain sight of Her Majesty's Palace. Rich, that's twenty-one you've spent in one sitting: a Customs House, a Jar and a Bowl. I'm sure the Harvest Pile is grateful. I've written it down.",
 "Clark: One piece is safe, and Rich has just bought two of the three I still need, which tells me exactly who I'm racing. The Gentry is the next man I deal with, and the ledger already has his page open.")
march({"W1":"I9", "W2":"N17"}, "Rob marches: W1 crosses the north river onto I9, in Rich's Province 3, and raids an empty square: 5 from Rich, and Rob pays Rich the 5 toll for the river. W2 moves along the desert to N17.",
 hover="The raid and the toll cancel: Rich stays at 26.5, still above the 20 his set needs.")
hire_soldier("G8", "Rob hires a third soldier, W3, for 20 to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on G8 in his desert, beside the strip under the Wall. His army is now at its limit of three. W3 moves from next year.")
say("Rob", "Rich, you said a respectful distance. I respect it very much, and I'm closing it one square at a time. That's five for the step and five back to you for the river, so call it a social visit. And the new man at G8 is only there to admire Clark's Land Deed, I promise.",
 "Rob: W1 crosses into Rich's province and pays for itself with the raid, and now it's three rounds from his Customs House. W2 slips along the desert toward the Jar, and the new soldier starts walking toward Clark's scroll.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official, as she declared. Her own reading of the I Ching is unpaid.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Rain and flood", "rainflood")
act("Reina turns the first I Ching card: Spring Floods. Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 collect a good harvest, Provinces 2, 4, 5 and 8 a bad one, and the population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "Spring Floods. 'Heaven waters the land generously. In some places, far too generously.' Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 have a good harvest; 2, 4, 5 and 8 have a bad one. The population holds at 100 million, multiple one, so read the x1 row. Clark, Silke, I'm sorry about the water. Rich, Paddy, the heavens smile on you this year, and I trust you'll remember the crown when they do.",
 "Reina: Rich has a full set on his fields and can take my seat whenever he likes, so his good harvest worries me more than Rob's dunes do. I'll keep building and keep the books spotless, so that taking the throne looks like a demotion to him.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Paddy, Your Majesty, the heavens were kind to us, and I'll remember the crown, I always do. The rent stays at five each this year, and Silke, I know the water hit you hard, so I won't ask you for a grain over the floor. Paddy, you had a lovely year. If you feel like showing a little gratitude to the man whose fields sit between you and the dunes, I'm open to talking about it. Rob, a social visit is very welcome. Do wipe your feet.",
 "Rich: I've got the full set and eighty-odd grain, so I could take the chair whenever I like. I'm keeping the rent gentle so both peasants owe me, because when I make my move I'll want their backing more than their five grain. Rob's soldier at I9 is three rounds off my Customs House, so I'm counting that too.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum again, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again. It's a share of what the heavens gave, so the flooded fields pay four and the fat ones pay twenty-four. That's just, by any book. The Grand Canal goes through Province 1, both sections at once, eighty from the crown alone. Clark, five for the collecting.",
 "Reina: Both canal sections add 20 a year to my harvest for the rest of the game, which pays for itself in four years and keeps the reserve healthy even if Rob never pays a grain. A rate on income hits the flooded provinces lightly, so 'excessive' is a hard charge to make stick.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 40% again: 4 each from Clark and Silke, whose fields flooded, and 24 each from Rich and Paddy, 56 in all; Rob refuses, and his income is nothing this year anyway. The crown will dig both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, for 80, and pay Clark his 5 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Before the Official collects, the table hears the rules of revolt (Section 16), put to Silke: a revolt can be called at any step. It needs its caller and two others agreeing on a cause, and the board card lists the causes. The Emperor may answer it, and it stands only if the caller and two others still back it. One called now, before the tax is paid, voids this year's tax. Under Chaos nobody pays tax, salaries or works, and nobody can buy new land; the harvest, the rent, the auctions and the Warlord carry on. The reserve is sealed: nobody spends it, and it passes to whoever is acclaimed. The throne then goes only by acclamation: anyone but the deposed Emperor may claim it, and the claims are counted on grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends.", map_hl=[5])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each owe this year's tax, and each is asked before the Official collects: pay it, or call a revolt, or say they'd back one. All four pay.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Four grain from a flooded field, paid on time and in full. Put it in the record, Your Majesty. That's forty percent two years running, which is getting close to 'consistently'. I'm not calling anything. I'm only saying I can count to three.",
 "Clark: A revolt today would only hand the throne to Rich, who has a full set and would swap into whatever chair is left warm. So I pay the four, log a second year of forty percent under 'excessive', and wait until the table owes me more than it owes him.")
say("Rich", "Twenty-four, gladly, Your Majesty. A share of what the heavens gave, just as you say, and a Grand Canal to show for it. I'd call that grain well spent. Clark, count it out in front of everyone. I like my generosity on the record. Silke, I wouldn't listen to talk of revolts in the second year. The crown's building, and builders deserve patience.",
 "Rich: Why would I back a revolt when I can take the chair myself whenever I like, with no Chaos and no sealed reserve? Let Reina spend her grain digging canals through her own province. Every work she finishes is one I'll inherit, so I'm paying with a smile and letting her build my palace for me.")
say("Silke", "Four from a flooded field. I'll pay it, Your Majesty, because for once the arithmetic is fair. But let's keep counting. Rich paid twenty-one into the pile at auction, holds a full set, and owes the crown twenty-four. Rob has three soldiers and pays nothing. Some of us are poorer than the ledger shows. Clark, write down that the Silk Peasant paid on time.",
 "Silke: A revolt now would only clear the way for Rich and his shiny new set to walk onto the throne, and I'm not handing him that for four grain. I'll keep 'taxes consistently excessive' in my pocket for a year when it costs me twenty-four.")
say("Paddy", "Twenty-four, counted out. It's a share of a good year, Your Majesty, and I'd sooner pay it than owe it. But a canal in Province 1 doesn't water a single paddy in Province 6, so when the works come round to the coast, I hope the crown remembers who paid in full in the flood year.",
 "Paddy: Forty percent of a fat harvest is fair, and I'm not starting a revolt I'd only end up owing someone for. What I'm watching is Rich: he's got a full set on his fields and he's just told me he's open to 'gratitude', and I didn't like the sound of that.")
taxes(.40, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: Reina digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace, which the Province 1 canal needs first, already stands. 790 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: Clark puts up his last Customs House, Rich his last Land Deed Scroll, Silke her last Dragon Jar and Paddy his last Wooden Bowl, and each sets the minimum. Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "These are the last of the Jars and Bowls, friends. I'll bid fair and firm on both, and I won't chase them into the Harvest Pile. Silke, Paddy, the Land Deed is all yours. I'm standing aside on it, and I'd take it kindly if the Jar and the Bowl went the same courteous way.",
 "Clark: If I win both at eleven or less I still have twenty in hand, and that's a full set, the same lever Rich is sitting on. Then the throne has two ladders against it instead of one.")
say("Rich", "I'm sitting most of this one out, friends. My shelves are full and I'm a contented man. My last Land Deed opens at three, same as before, fair and modest. Silke, Paddy, the Customs House is all yours to fight over, though I'd settle it between you rather than feed the pile. Clark, you're always writing things down. I do hope there's room in that ledger for a Jar and a Bowl.",
 "Rich: One full set on this table is power, and two is competition. Clark already holds the Land Deed, so a Jar and a Bowl would put him one step from my chair. I'll pay a little over the odds to keep him a collector instead of a contender.")
say("Silke", "Last of everything, so let's be plain. Paddy has sixty-three and a half, I have thirty-three and a half, and we both need the same Customs House and the same Deed. Paddy, you want my Dragon Jar and I want your Bowl. If you stand aside on one of the papers, I'll stand aside on the other, and three-quarters of a bidding war doesn't go into the pile. Say so now. Otherwise I bid, and my Jar opens at eight.",
 "Silke: These are the last pieces in the box. If Paddy takes the Customs House or the Deed, my set is dead for good and I'm a silk farmer forever, so I'm spending real grain this time. And he's the only one who needs my Jar, so he can pay for it.")
say("Paddy", "These are the last pieces in the box, so I'll say it straight: I'm after all three, and I'd rather not hand the Harvest Pile three-quarters of a bidding war to get them. Silke, Clark, if you stand aside I'll owe you, and I pay what I owe. My Bowl goes up at two, same as ever.",
 "Paddy: With the stock run out, whoever doesn't finish a set now never will, and Rich already has his. I've the most grain of the peasants, so I'm bidding firm on all three, but not so high that I can't keep the twenty a set needs.")
say("Rob", "Passing again. I'm a simple man of the sands. Silke, Paddy, buy yourselves something pretty this year, you've earned it. And Clark, I can count to three as well. Forty, forty, and we'll see. I'm only saying that when the time comes, the desert votes.",
 "Rob: Silke and Paddy have nothing on the board yet, so I can't take a piece from them until they buy one. I'm cheering their shopping and leaving Clark's revolt talk to cook, because a table with no Emperor is my kind of table.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed Scroll 3, Dragon Jar 8, Wooden Bowl 2. Rob passes on every lot; Rich bids only to keep the Jar and the Bowl from Clark. On the Customs House and the Land Deed, Silke's limit is one step over Paddy's, so each of them gets one live raise on both.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Silke", "Clark has the last Bowl, so my set is finished before it started. Fine. But Paddy, you won't get yours by outspending a flooded silk farmer either. Thirteen on the Customs House, and everything I have left on the Deed. If you want to climb past the coast, it'll cost you double, and three-quarters of it goes into the pile.",
 "Silke: I can't complete a set now, but Paddy needs both of these papers, so if I hold even one of them he's stuck in the paddies with me. That makes him a peasant who needs allies, and a peasant who needs allies backs a revolt.")
say("Paddy", "Silke, the last Bowl's gone to Clark, so neither paper finishes anything for you now. All a war buys either of us is a fuller Harvest Pile. I'll go to eighteen on each, and that's my last word. Let me have them and I'll owe you, and you know I pay what I owe.",
 "Paddy: I've got the Jar, so these two papers are the only thing between me and a full set. I can't outbid Silke on both if she's set on spite, so I'm going firm and asking her to look at the arithmetic.")
DC = "Noted, with the price. The ledger has a long memory."
DR = "Well bid, Clark. Enjoy the porcelain."
DS = "Fine, Paddy, the coast can have it. Just remember I'm the one who counted what it cost you."
DP = "Fair enough, it's yours. I'll not throw good rice after it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,13), B(5,None,DS), B(6,14)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. After the live raise Silke stood at 13 and Paddy at 18. Paddy puts the Customs House on S25.", at="S25")
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,18), B(6,None,DP), B(5,19)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. After the live raise Paddy stood at 18 and Silke at 20. Silke puts the Land Deed Scroll on P26.", at="P26")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,11), B(2,None,DC), B(6,12)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 8. Clark stopped at 11; Paddy's limit was 14. Paddy puts the Dragon Jar on S24.", at="S24")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,8), B(5,None,DS), B(3,9), B(3,None,DR), B(2,10)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 8; Rich bid only to keep it from Clark, up to 9; Clark's limit was 11. Clark puts the Wooden Bowl on H16.", at="H16")
act("Every province's stock is sold. Clark holds a Land Deed Scroll and a Wooden Bowl and needs a Dragon Jar; Paddy holds a Customs House and a Dragon Jar and needs a Land Deed Scroll; Silke holds a Land Deed Scroll. Rich's is still the only complete set.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Paddy", "The Customs House and the Jar go down at the far end of the coast, by the water, as far from the sand as Province 6 goes. Silke, you've got the Deed, fair and square. When you're ready to talk about it, I'll be here, and I'll be fair.",
 "Paddy: Two of three, tucked into the corner where Rob's lads can't reach them for a good while. Silke has the Deed I need and no set to use it in, so this isn't over. It's a conversation for next year, and she'll set the price.")
say("Silke", "Nineteen for a scroll that finishes nothing for me. I know, Paddy, I can count. It goes on P26, as far from the dunes as the coast allows. Paddy, you're one Deed short and the box is empty, so whenever you want to talk about it, the silk farm is open for business.",
 "Silke: I've got fourteen and a half grain left and the only Land Deed Paddy can ever get. That paper is worth more as a bargaining chip than it ever was in a set.")
say("Clark", "The Bowl goes on H16, beside the Deed and well back from Rob's sightseers. Paddy, thank you for the Bowl. You beat me to the Jar fair and square, and I don't hold that against a man, though I do write it down. The box is empty now, friends, so anyone with a spare Jar and an interest in the Official's goodwill knows where I sit.",
 "Clark: The auction box is empty, so my last piece has to come from a deal: Rich's Jar on L13, or Paddy's new one. Paddy owes favours all round the table, and I mean to be the one he pays first.")
march({"W1":"J10", "W2":"O18", "W3":"G9"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto J10 in Rich's Province 3 and raids an empty square, 5 from Rich. W2 steps back to O18, the desert beside Silke's coast, and W3 onto G9, the strip under the Wall.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations, everyone, the box is empty and the shelves are full. That's the nicest thing you could have done for me. Rich, I've wiped my feet at J10, and that's five for the doormat. Paddy, Silke, I'm sure the coast is lovely this time of year.",
 "Rob: W1 is two steps from Rich's Customs House, W3 has slipped onto the strip under the Wall on its way to Clark's scroll, and W2 starts the long walk toward the coast. Paddy put his pieces as far from the sand as he could, and that only tells me which way to walk.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official, as she declared. Her own reading of the I Ching is unpaid.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: The Right Afternoon
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "A careless year", "careless")
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Careless Prosperity. Every province collects a good harvest, and every player plays the first personal disaster in their booklet. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "\"Careless Prosperity. Good times make careless people.\" Every province brings in a good harvest, and every one of us, me included, plays the next Personal Disaster in our booklet. Population holds at 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row. Let's not be careless with a good year.",
 "Reina: A good harvest across the board is exactly the kind of year when a dynasty builds, and it lets me ease the rate a touch without anyone calling it a retreat. What I'm watching is how the disasters land, Rich's especially, because a man with a full set and a bad night is a man who starts eyeing my chair.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(1, "a rare horse, sent up the Silk Road as tribute, throws the Emperor in her excitement to ride it.")
disaster(2, "on an inspection of the granaries, the Official's cart drops a wheel and runs into a ditch.")
disaster(3, "the family's head cook retires after forty years, and the Gentry gives him the send-off he's owed.")
disaster(5, "a storm off the coast wrecks the boat the Silk Peasant's sons take out to the mulberry islands, and a new one must be built.")
disaster(6, "the Rice Peasant's only water buffalo breaks its leg, and the planting stops while another is found.")
disaster(8, "the Warlord is wounded in the legs in his last skirmish. No raids on Provinces 5, 6 or 7 this year.")
say("Rich", "A good harvest for everyone, sixty to every province, and a grand Careless Prosperity to Her Majesty's reign! Silke, Paddy: five each, the floor and not a grain over. In a year this good I'd be ashamed to squeeze you. Just remember which landlord kept it at five when he could have asked for twenty.",
 "Rich: Every peasant is owed sixty this year and I'm asking for five. That's not charity, it's a deposit, because if this ever comes down to acclamation they'll count friends and I want two in my column already. Meanwhile I'm sitting on 127 grain and the only complete set at the table, so all I need now is the right afternoon.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum again, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Thirty percent this year. In a careless year the crown can afford to take a smaller share, so call it the harvest's generosity, not mine. And the Winter Palace goes up this year, which opens the Grand Canal to the coast. Silke, Paddy: your sections come next. Clark, your five, as always.",
 "Reina: Dropping to thirty takes the word \"consistently\" out of Clark's mouth without me admitting anything, and the Winter Palace is the promise to the coast that they can see being built. If I give the peasants canals to wait for, they have a reason not to lend Rich the three voices he needs.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 30%: 18 each from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy, 72 in all; Rob refuses his 6. The crown will build the Winter Palace alone, for 20, and pay Clark his 5 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Before the Official collects, a point of the rules is put to Clark: Rich's set can take the throne at any step while there's an Emperor on it. Once a revolt stands, no set can: under Chaos the throne passes only by acclamation, so Rich would have to win the count on grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends, like any other claimant. A revolt is the one thing that turns a complete set back into three pieces on a shelf.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Eighteen, paid on the nail, Your Majesty. And thirty percent, look at that, so the 'forty percent' page in my book stays at two years and not three. I'll write it in as the harvest's generosity, just as you asked. Rob, I see you've turned down the crown's six for the third year running. Don't worry, I'm keeping a note of it. I keep a note of everything.",
 "Clark: She cut the tax just before it counted as 'consistently excessive.' Clever woman, and she's ruined my best cause. But the Warlord page is three years thick now, and the Storyteller just told me the one thing I needed to know. A revolt turns Rich's full set back into three pots on a shelf, so I'll call one when I have two signatures on it and not before.")
say("Rich", "Eighteen, paid on the nail, Your Majesty, and thirty percent was very gracious of you. And because I so admire the way you've run things, I'd like to carry on the work myself. Customs House, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl and twenty grain: I'm presenting my set for the throne. The Winter Palace will still go up, Reina, and you'll be first on the guest list.",
 "Rich: Clark was writing notes and getting a lot of advice, and I could feel a revolt coming, which would turn my set into three pots on a shelf. So I paid my tax like an honest man and took the chair before anyone else could speak. This was the right afternoon.")
act("Rich pays his 18 to the crown first.", deltas={pid(3):-18, pid(1):18}, map_hl=[1,3])
swap(3, 1, "Rich, holding a full set and 119 grain, changes seats with Reina and takes the throne. He takes the crown's whole reserve, 216, for 335 in all. Becoming Emperor returns all his pieces to their provinces' stock: the Customs House to Province 2, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6, to be auctioned again. Reina moves to Province 3 as the Gentry, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="The tax has been declared but not collected, so the crown's year passes to the new Emperor: he may keep the rate or change it, and he chooses the year's work and the salaries. Province 3's tax for the year is paid.")
say("Rich", "My friends, my first act: the tax drops to twenty percent. Clark, Silke, Paddy, twelve each, and Rob four if he feels patriotic. The Winter Palace goes up this year as Reina promised, so the Canal can reach the coast next year, and Clark, your salary is doubled to ten. A rich crown doesn't need to squeeze, it needs to be liked.",
 "Rich: With 335 in the reserve, nobody can say it's too low, and at twenty percent nobody can say the taxes are excessive, so I've taken away two of the revolt causes on my first afternoon. The ten for Clark buys me the one man at this table who's been reading up on revolts.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 20% for the rest of the table: 12 each from Clark, Silke and Paddy, 36 in all; Rob may refuse his 4. The crown will build the Winter Palace alone, for 20, and pay Clark 10 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Clark, Silke and Paddy each owe the new Emperor's tax, and each is asked before the Official collects: pay it, or call a revolt, or say they'd back one. Clark was asked again, since the throne changed hands after his first answer. All three pay.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Clark", "Twelve, paid, Your new Majesty, and thank you for the raise. Ten a year buys a lot of ink. I'll enter it all: Year Three, the crown changes hands in the middle of the tax, the rate goes down and the Official's pay goes up. A very good page. And I hear there's a Dragon Jar back on the auction block this year, so nobody get too attached to it.",
 TH(("Clark", "Rich has the crown, which means his set is gone, and that Jar he gave back to stock is the one piece I'm missing. I'll buy it, then hold the Deed, the Bowl and the Jar while Rich sits on the throne thinking he's safe."),
    ("Clark, whispering to Reina", "You were thrown off your own throne in the middle of your own tax year, and I wrote it down. When I make my move, I want your name next to mine. Back me and the Gentry's chair stays with you, with no questions about rent.")))
say("Silke", "Twelve, paid. Twenty percent is a number I can live with, Your Majesty, so enjoy it while it lasts. Let me count us, though: Rich three hundred thirty-five, Clark ninety-seven, Paddy ninety-one, Rob sixty-five, Reina sixty, and me fifty-six and a half after this. So one man holds more than the rest of the table put together. And a Customs House and a Wooden Bowl just went back on the block, which happen to be the two things standing between me and a full set. Bid high on them, Rich, and I'll know exactly what the crown thinks of the coast.",
 "Silke: I pay because twenty is fair, and a revolt I call over a fair tax is one that nobody backs. That Customs House and that Bowl are my way back into the game, so I'm saving every grain for the auction. Rich just took the reserve and the throne in one go, and that's the sort of thing people remember when I need two of them to agree with me.")
act("The count, for the record: once the tax is paid the other five hold 369.5 between them, more than Rich's 335. And the Emperor doesn't bid at auction.", map_hl=[1])
say("Paddy", "Twelve, paid, Your Majesty, and gladly. Twenty percent is fair and I've no quarrel with a fair number. I'll just say that a canal that stops at the Winter Palace waters nobody's rice, so I'll be watching for those coast sections next year like you promised.",
 "Paddy: I'd sooner pay it than owe it, and there's no revolt in me over a low tax. Rich holding the whole crown's purse does worry me, though, so I'll keep my grain and save my voice for the day he breaks a promise to the coast.")
taxes(.20, skip=(3, 8), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="G21", text="Public works: Rich builds the Winter Palace on G21, in Province 1, the square Reina chose. It must stand before the canals in Provinces 4, 5 and 6.", hover="Rich pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 770 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: the three pieces Rich returned are sold this year. Clark puts up the Customs House, Silke the Dragon Jar and Paddy the Wooden Bowl, and each sets the minimum. Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Clark", "The Jar is the one piece I've been chasing for three years, and you all watched me lose the last one to Paddy by a single grain. Reina, I'll put it to you in public: let me have the Jar, and the next Customs House or Bowl that comes up is yours without a bid from me. Every grain we spend fighting goes three-quarters into the Pile and helps nobody. The Customs House goes at the floor, two, and Silke, I'd be pleased to see it on the coast.",
 "Clark: Win the Jar and I'm still holding twenty-three or more, which completes my set, and once the set is complete every chair at this table is open to me, including Rich's. Selling the Customs House cheap and passing on the Bowl hands Silke her own set, and a second complete set aimed at a fat Emperor is a friend I want to have.")
say("Reina", "Clark, done, in public and on your own page: the Jar is yours, and the next Customs House or Bowl is mine. Rich, congratulations. You've paid for one year of applause. I built the dynasty's first three works, and I can count the years until I do it again.",
 TH(("Reina", "I've just been thrown out of my own chair, and the quickest way back is the same door Rich used: a full set. So I take Clark's truce on the Jar and keep enough grain for the twenty in hand. Silke is the only one who'll fight me for the House and the Bowl, and she has less to spend than I do."),
    ("Reina, whispering to Clark", "I don't sign blank pages. Show me the cause and the moment, and if it's sound you'll have my name beside yours. The rent can stay a matter for the Gentry.")))
say("Silke", "Let me count this one out loud too. Reina, you hold nothing now, and Clark's taking the Jar, so you can't finish a set this year whatever you buy. I can. A Customs House and a Bowl on the coast make me a set, and every grain you spend running me up goes three-quarters into the Pile. Stand aside on these two and I'll stay out of the next Jar or Deed that comes up, and you can put that on Clark's page next to the other promise. The Jar starts at six, Clark. You've chased it for three years, so it's worth six.",
 "Silke: I can spend thirty-six on the two pieces and still keep the twenty grain the set needs, and whatever Clark pays for my Jar comes a quarter back to me. If Reina takes the deal I'm one step from climbing out of the paddy field, and if she doesn't, I've made her pay for my patience in front of everyone.")
say("Paddy", "I'm out of all three. I've a Jar and a Customs House already and no use for another, and I'll not push up a price just to watch three-quarters of it go into the Pile. The Bowl goes at two, the floor. Silke, if it ends up on the coast I'll be glad of it. All I ask, in front of everyone, is that when you've a Deed you can spare, or the next one comes up, I get the first fair word on it.",
 "Paddy: Leaving the Bowl at the floor costs me almost nothing and puts Silke in my debt for once, not the other way round. The only thing I need is a Deed, and the person most likely to hand me one is the neighbour I've just been kind to.")
say("Rob", "Pass, pass and pass. Buy whatever you like, friends. Every piece you put on the board is one more thing for me to come and visit. And congratulations on the throne, Your Majesty. Three hundred and fifty grain and not one soldier. I'd sleep with a lamp on.",
 "Rob: Why would I pay for realia when I can collect it for nothing? The more of this table's treasure ends up on the board this year, the better my walk next year. And Rich just took the throne holding a full purse and no army, so the next time Clark says 'the Emperor can't control the Warlord', I'd like that to be true.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Dragon Jar 6, Wooden Bowl 2. Paddy and Rob pass on every lot; Reina stands aside on the Jar, as she promised Clark. On the Bowl Reina's limit and Silke's are both 16, and a tie goes by seat order to Reina, so each of them gets one live raise.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Reina", "Silke, it's a generous offer, but you're asking me to hand you a set in exchange for a promise about a Jar that hasn't come up yet. I won't trade a piece on the table for one in the air. The Bowl stays in Province 3 if I can keep it there.",
 "Reina: If Silke gets this Bowl she has a full set and a key to any chair at the table, possibly the one I want back. At twenty-four she'd have to spend her twenty in hand to beat me, so she can't finish this year either way.")
say("Silke", "Twenty-one, and that's every grain I can spare, Reina. You know it, because I've just counted it out loud. Take the Bowl off me and you've paid twenty-two for a quarter of a set you can't finish. Let it go and you'll have a peasant on the upper benches who owes you one.",
 "Silke: Forty-one in hand, twenty kept back for the set, so twenty-one is my ceiling to the grain. If Reina outbids that, she's burned her grain on spite and I'm still holding the Deed and the Customs House for next year.")
DRe = "Take it, then. The coast needs its pieces as much as I do, and I'll find mine."
DS = "Fine, take it, and enjoy what it cost you. I'll still be here when the next piece comes up."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,16), B(3,None,DRe), B(5,17)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 2. Reina stopped at 16; Silke's limit was 20. Silke puts the Customs House on P25.", at="P25")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,6)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 6. Nobody bid against Clark: Reina stood aside, as she promised. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on I15.", at="I15")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,21), B(5,None,DS), B(3,22)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. After the live raise Silke stood at 21 and Reina at 24. Reina puts the Wooden Bowl on L16.", at="L16")
say("Clark", "Six grain, and three years of chasing, paid off. Thank you, Silke, and thank you, Reina; your promise goes in the book in your own words, and so does mine. Next Customs House or Bowl, I don't bid. Rob, I've put it well back from the Wall, so don't bother bringing your lantern.",
 "Clark: Deed, Bowl, Jar, and more than eighty grain in hand: that's a complete set, so from this moment I can step into any chair at this table, the throne included. Rich has three hundred and fifty grain, no soldiers and a crown that changes hands whenever someone presents a set, so I'll present mine when it suits me, and Reina says she'll sign once I show her the moment.")
act("Clark holds a Land Deed Scroll, a Wooden Bowl and a Dragon Jar, the three pieces his set needs, and 83 grain: a complete set. He may change places with anyone at any moment, the throne included. He holds it for now.", map_hl=[2], big=True)
say("Silke", "P25, next to my Deed, as far from the desert as the coast allows. Reina, twenty-two for a Bowl, against a Customs House and a Deed you don't have. Count it yourself: you're one piece into a set that needs four, and I'm two into one that needs three. The next Bowl that comes up, I'll be waiting for it, and I'll have the grain.",
 "Silke: The Customs House sits next to the Land Deed on P26, out of every soldier's reach for two years, so now I only need one Wooden Bowl. Reina paid twenty-two to stop me, which means she's the one I'm watching, and the one who has to keep winning that piece every time it comes up.")
act("For the record: at six players the Gentry's set is three pieces, a Customs House, a Dragon Jar and a Wooden Bowl. Reina holds one of the three.", map_hl=[3])
say("Reina", "The Bowl goes on L16, well back from the desert. Silke, it isn't personal. I've been building things at this table since Year One, and I start again with this.",
 "Reina: L16 is out of every soldier's reach for two rounds, so Rob can wave at it all he likes. Now I have a piece in hand, 38 grain, and a Customs House and a Jar still to find, and I'll be watching who sells them.")
march({"W1":"I11", "W2":"N17", "W3":"G10"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto I11 in Reina's Province 3 and raids an empty square, 5 from Reina. W2 steps back to N17 in the desert and W3 onto G10, the strip under the Wall; neither raids. His wound bars raids on Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year.")
say("Rob", "Five from Province 3, Reina, just for the road. Nothing personal: you said it yourself, you're starting again, and I like to be there at the start. And Clark, a full set, well done! Funny thing about a set, though. It only takes one missing piece to turn it back into a shelf of crockery.",
 "Rob: Clark is sitting on a complete set, with the Scroll four steps from W1 and the Jar five, so W1 and W3 are both heading for the Wall to break it before he can use it. W2 is creeping up on Reina's new Bowl, three rounds out, and the coast can wait until my leg heals.")
salaries({2:10}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Clark 10 as the Official, as he declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "The treasure chest", "chest")
act("Rich turns the year's I Ching card himself: Imperial Charity. Every province collects an average harvest, and the Emperor gives every player 2 from the reserve. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1])
say("Rich", "Imperial Charity! 'Moved by the suffering of his people, the Emperor opens his purse. Slightly.' I'll take 'slightly' as a compliment to my thrift. Every province has an average harvest, and every one of you gets 2 from my reserve, gladly given. The population holds at 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row. And from next year, Silke, the card is yours to read, at five a year: you count so well out loud that the crown ought to be paying for it.",
 "Rich: Clark is sitting on a full set and can take this chair from me whenever he likes, so this year every grain I spend is spent on friends. Five a year turns Silke, the loudest counter at the table, into a crown employee.")
act("Imperial Charity: Rich gives Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob 2 each from the crown's reserve, 10 in all. From next year Silke reads the I Ching as the Emperor's appointee, at 5 a year.", deltas={pid(1):-10, pid(2):2, pid(3):2, pid(5):2, pid(6):2, pid(8):2}, map_hl=[1])
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Thirty-five to every province, as the card gives it. Silke, Paddy: five each, the rent the book sets. I didn't buy anyone's loyalty from the throne, and I won't start from the Gentry's chair. And Clark, congratulations on your set. Everyone at this table now knows you can sit anywhere you like.",
 "Reina: I ask the floor and nothing more, so no peasant has a grievance against me, and I say Clark's set out loud so the whole table counts it alongside Rich's. A man holding a key to every chair is a better target than a woman holding one Bowl.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina, now the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays her 5.")
say("Rich", "Twenty percent again, my friends. I said a rich crown needn't squeeze, and I meant it for more than one year. Paddy, you asked me to bring the water to the coast and I'm bringing it: both sections of the Province 6 canal this year, eighty grain, and the crown pays every grain. Silke, your sections are next, and as my Scholar you'll be here to remind me. Clark, your pay goes up to fifteen. A good Official is worth a good wage.",
 "Rich: Eighty grain on Paddy's rice is the best thing the reserve can buy me: he gets plus twenty a year for good, and he can't say the works have been delayed while I build them. Fifteen to Clark makes sitting with his set worth more than using it, at least for this year.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 20%: 7 each from Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy, 28 in all; Rob may refuse his 2. The crown will dig both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 6, for 80, and pay Clark 15 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Clark", "Your Majesty, a fifteen-grain salary is very generous, and I'm afraid I have to turn it down, because I'd rather pay it. Deed, Bowl, Jar and twenty grain: I'm presenting my set for the throne. Nothing changes but the man in the chair. The tax stays at twenty, Paddy gets both canal sections this year as promised, and Silke stays on as Scholar. Rich, you'll make an excellent Official. I've kept the ledger warm for you.",
 TH(("Clark", "Rob was three rounds from breaking up my set, and the crown's chest is the fattest it's been all game, so I'm not going to sit on a full hand waiting for a better moment. I've kept every one of Rich's promises to the table, which means nobody at this table has a reason to revolt against me, and the people with a reason to back me already know who they are."),
    ("Clark, whispering to Reina", "This is the moment you asked to be shown. I want your name beside mine if Rich tries to talk the table into a revolt, and in return the next public work the crown builds goes in Province 3, under the Wall where you've always wanted it.")))
swap(2, 1, "Clark, holding a full set and 130 grain, changes seats with Rich and takes the throne. He takes the crown's whole reserve, 366, for 496 in all. Becoming Emperor returns all his pieces to their provinces' stock: the Land Deed Scroll to Province 3, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6, to be auctioned again. Rich moves to Province 2 as the Official, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="As in Year 3, the tax has been declared but not collected, so the crown's year passes to the new Emperor. Clark keeps Rich's 20% and the Province 6 canal; Province 2's 7 is now Rich's to pay, and as the Official he collects. Silke stays the appointee for the I Ching. The Official's salary is Clark's to set.")
act("Rich, Reina, Silke and Paddy each owe this year's tax, and each is asked before the Official collects: pay it, or call a revolt, or say they'd back one. All four pay.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Rich", "Seven, paid on the nail, Your Majesty, and very neatly done. I couldn't have taken it better myself, and I'd know. The ledger is in good hands, Clark, and the tax will come in to the last grain, as it always did with you. Though I'll say the Official's salary was fifteen when I set it.",
 "Rich: One year in the chair and I've learned the lesson I taught everyone else: a set is a key, and Clark had the other one. Three pieces of Official's crockery go to auction this year, so I pay my seven, smile, and get back to collecting.")
say("Reina", "Seven, paid. Twenty percent is a fair rate, and I don't call revolts over fair numbers. Congratulations, Clark. That's three Emperors in four years, so whoever holds the chair should build fast enough that people would miss them.",
 TH(("Reina", "A revolt now would turn every set into crockery on a shelf, mine included, and a Dragon Jar has just gone back on the block. I want this table quiet and auctioning, not in Chaos."),
    ("Reina, whispering to Clark", "Agreed, as long as the work is real. The Province 3 Reservoir or links of the Wall above me, built in your first full year. Do that and you'll have my name beside yours against any revolt Rich tries to talk up.")))
say("Silke", "Seven, paid. Twenty is fair, and I'll say so as easily as I'd say forty wasn't. Three Emperors in four years, and none of them from the coast. Now let me count the auction: a Wooden Bowl's going back on the block. Reina already has hers, Clark is sitting on the throne, and Clark gave his word on his own page not to bid on the next Bowl. That Bowl is the last piece I need. Anyone who bids against me had better want it more than I do, and nobody here does.",
 "Silke: I pay the seven because a fair tax isn't worth a revolt, and I'm saving my grain for that Bowl. Seventy-three in hand less seven leaves me sixty-six, so I can go to forty-six and still keep the twenty the set needs. The only person who might bid against me now is Paddy, and he asked me for a fair word.")
say("Paddy", "Seven, paid, Your Majesty, and thank you for keeping the canal for Province 6. That's the first time anyone's promised me water and the promise lasted past the change of chair. I'll say it plainly now so nobody's surprised later: there's a Land Deed Scroll on the block this year, it's the one piece I lack, and I'll be bidding for it honestly. I won't touch anyone else's lot.",
 "Paddy: Water's coming to my rice, so the crown has my seven and my thanks. The Deed coming back to market is the chance I've waited three years for, and I've saved my grain for exactly this.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Rich, now the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={6:2}, text="Public works: Clark digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 6, at the crown's cost of 80, as Rich declared. Each adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="Clark pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the coast's canals need first, stands. 690 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: the three pieces Clark returned are sold this year. Reina puts up the Land Deed Scroll (Province 3's stock, now hers to sell), Silke the Dragon Jar and Paddy the Wooden Bowl, and each sets the minimum. Rich, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3,5,6])
say("Rich", "Congratulations to all of you on such a well-run auction, and to His Majesty on a very tidy afternoon. I'll be honest with you, the way an Official ought to be: I need all three of these, I've got fifty-three grain, and I won't pour it into the Pile in a bidding war. I'll bid what I think each piece is worth and no more, and nobody here should take it personally.",
 "Rich: Paddy has twice my grain and Silke has her heart set on the Bowl, so the only piece I can realistically get is the Jar, and that's the one I'm really bidding on. Once a set gets used, those pieces come back on the block, and that's when I'll need my grain, so I'm keeping it.")
say("Reina", "The Deed goes up at the floor, two. Paddy, you said you'd bid honestly for it, so I won't make it cost you more than that. The Jar is the one I'm after, and I'll say so as plainly as Rich did. The Bowl I leave to whoever wants it.",
 "Reina: Rich has fifty-three and needs three pieces plus twenty in hand, so he can't afford to chase me hard on the Jar. If I take it at thirty-five or less I'm one Customs House away from a set and still have grain left.")
say("Silke", "The Deed's Paddy's. I'm not bidding on it, and that's your fair word, Paddy, given in public as you asked. The Jar starts at eight; Reina, you said you're after it, so I've counted it for you. And the Bowl: Rich, you've fifty-three grain and three pieces to buy. Spend it all on the Bowl if you like, but then you'll have no Deed and no Jar, and I'll still have a set. I'm going as high as my twenty-grain reserve allows.",
 "Silke: Forty-six on the Bowl leaves me exactly the twenty the set needs, and Rich can't match that and still buy anything else. If I take the Bowl, I've got a full set and a seat on the upper benches, and three Emperors in four years tells me the throne isn't hard to reach.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Reina, and thank you, Silke. That's two fair words kept, and I'll remember both. Rich, I'm not out to cost you anything, but I've waited three years on this Deed and I'll go as high as I have to for it. The Jar's not mine to want, and the Bowl goes at the floor again, two, same as last year.",
 "Paddy: Rich has fifty-three grain at most, so a ceiling of sixty takes the Deed and still leaves me well over the twenty I need to finish the set. Fair is fair: the Bowl stays at two so Silke can finish hers.")
say("Rob", "Pass on all three. Clark, I walked halfway across Province 3 to visit your set, and you went and sat on it. Very rude. Go on, the rest of you, buy them up and put them on the board. I'll be by shortly.",
 "Rob: That's three Emperors in four years, all of them bought with crockery, and I'd rather keep my eighty-two grain for the day this table stops swapping chairs and starts counting grain and soldiers. W2 is two steps from Reina's Bowl, so the hunt goes on.")
act("The minimums: Land Deed Scroll 2, Dragon Jar 8, Wooden Bowl 2. Rob passes on every lot. No lot is close enough at the top for a live raise.", map_hl=[3,5,6])
DRi = "Take it, with my blessing. The Official will be at the next auction too."
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,10), B(2,None,DRi), B(6,11)],
 hover="Reina's minimum was 2. Rich stopped at 10; Paddy's limit was 60. Paddy puts the Land Deed Scroll on S23.", at="S23")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,14), B(2,None,DRi), B(3,15)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 8. Rich stopped at 14; Reina's limit was 35. Reina puts the Dragon Jar on K17.", at="K17")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,10), B(2,None,DRi), B(5,11)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Rich stopped at 10; Silke's limit was 46. Silke puts the Wooden Bowl on P24.", at="P24")
say("Paddy", "Eleven grain, and I'll not pretend I'm not pleased. It goes on S23, right next to the Jar and the Customs House, down on the bottom of the coast with the rest of my things. Rob, it's a long walk, and the canal's going to be in your way.",
 "Paddy: That's a full set: Deed, Customs House, Jar, and plenty over the twenty grain. I didn't owe anyone a grain to get here. Now I've got a choice I never expected to have, and I mean to think hard before I make it.")
say("Reina", "K17, next to the Bowl and well back from the desert. Fifteen grain, and thank you, Silke, for the price. Rob, you're welcome to come and look at it, if you can walk that far.",
 "Reina: Bowl and Jar sit side by side, out of reach for two rounds, and I still have sixty-odd grain. One Customs House and I have a set again, and Silke and Paddy have both just finished theirs, so this table is about to get very crowded with people who can take a chair.")
say("Silke", "P24, in a row with the Customs House and the Deed, so the coast finally has a full set. Deed, Customs House, Bowl and well over twenty grain, so count it if you like, Clark. I'm not presenting it yet. Rich, Reina, a peasant with a full set is a very polite guest, until the day she isn't.",
 "Silke: Eleven grain for the last piece, so I've got a full set and about fifty-eight in hand, and I can climb into the Official's or the Gentry's chair whenever it pays most. Rich is sitting in the Official's chair with nothing, so that's the one I'm watching, and whoever wants me to stay put can make me an offer.")
act("Paddy holds a Customs House, a Dragon Jar and a Land Deed Scroll and 101 grain; Silke a Land Deed Scroll, a Customs House and a Wooden Bowl and 58.5. Both are complete sets: each may replace the Official, the Gentry or the other peasant at any moment, though not the Emperor. Both hold them for now.", map_hl=[5,6], big=True)
march({"W1":"J12", "W2":"M16", "W3":"G11"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto J12 in Rich's Province 2 and raids an empty square, 5 from Rich. W2 moves along the desert to M16, beside Reina's Bowl, and W3 to G11 on the strip under the Wall; neither raids.")
say("Rob", "Five from Province 2, Rich, a little welcome to your new chair. And Reina, you put the Jar and the Bowl next to each other. That's very thoughtful of you: I'll only have to make the one trip.",
 "Rob: W2 is now one step from Reina's Bowl and two from her Jar, and W1 is closing in from the Wall side, so next march one of them gets taken into the desert. W3 is strolling south towards the palaces, because a raid there costs Clark twenty, and an Emperor who can't protect his own palace is a fine cause for a revolt.")
say("Clark", "Fifteen, Rich. You set it, and I've been on the receiving end of a promise that died with the chair, so I'm not going to do that to you. Here's next year in public so nobody has to guess: the Province 3 Reservoir, for Reina, who built the first three works and deserves a fourth. Silke, Paddy, two full sets on the coast is noted. I'm glad you're polite guests, because this crown means to be a good host.",
 "Clark: Paying an Official I've just pushed out of the throne fifteen grain costs me less than one bad rumour, and an Official who isn't broke can't be turned into an 'in debt' cause for a revolt. The Reservoir buys me Reina's signature, and keeping the tax at twenty and getting the canals built keeps both coastal sets aimed at each other's chairs and away from mine.")
salaries({2:15}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Rich 15 as the Official, the amount Rich had set. Silke reads the I Ching from next year, at 5 a year.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Fifteen of Every Twenty

R(5, "Drought", "blight")
act("Silke turns the year's I Ching card as the Emperor's appointee: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Silke", "\"Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year. All provinces collect an average harvest.\" So nobody's flooded and nobody's lucky. Population holds at one hundred million, times one, so read the x1 row, everyone. And Clark, I'll remind you the reader's fee was five, and a dry year is no excuse to forget it.",
 "Silke: An average year, with Clark sitting on four hundred and twenty-nine and the Pile at seventeen hundred, so this year the tax has no weather to hide behind. If Rich comes asking for forty percent of an average harvest, I'll have a full set and a cause in my pocket, and his chair is the one I'm measuring.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Thirty-five to everyone, fifty-five to Paddy's rice and ten to the desert. A dry year, so it's an average year and an average share. Rent stays at the floor, five each, the same as last year. I said I wouldn't squeeze the coast, and I don't change my word with the weather. Clark, Silke's right about her fee. Pay the reader, it's cheaper than a grudge.",
 "Reina: Five from each of them keeps the peasants quiet and keeps them owing me a little goodwill, and when the chair goes to acclamation, goodwill counts as much as grain. All I need now is a Customs House, and I'd rather Silke and Paddy didn't feel like selling theirs to someone else out of spite.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays her 5.")
say("Clark", "Twenty percent again, and a dry year won't change it. That's three years at twenty under this chair, so put that on whatever page you like. The Province 3 Reservoir goes in this year, all eighty from the crown, just as I promised. Rich, your fifteen stands. Silke, your five is in the ledger and paid. I don't forget a debt, mine or anyone else's.",
 "Clark: Every promise kept to the letter and every cause for revolt crossed off in front of witnesses. Eighty grain from a 464 reserve buys me Reina's name in ink, and twenty a year in her fields reminds her who dug them. Rich gets his fifteen and nothing to complain about, and I'm watching Rob's soldiers.")
act("Clark declares a tax of 20%: 7 each from Rich, Reina and Silke and 11 from Paddy; Rob may refuse his 2. The crown will build the Province 3 Reservoir, all 80 from the reserve, and at the year's end pay Rich 15 as the Official and Silke 5 for reading the I Ching.", map_hl=[1,3])
act("Rich, Reina, Silke and Paddy each owe this year's tax, and each is asked before the Official collects: pay it, or call a revolt, or say they'd back one.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Rich", "Seven grain, Your Majesty, and gladly. Twenty percent in a drought is the kindest tax this table has seen, and I'd know, since I set the last one. I'll go round and collect it now, every grain counted twice, and I'll thank you in advance for the fifteen.",
 "Rich: The Storyteller says a sealed reserve of four hundred and sixty-four goes to whoever gets acclaimed, and that's very tempting, but Paddy has a hundred and fifty-one in hand to my ninety-eight, and I have no soldiers and no cause anyone would believe. I don't start a revolt I can't win. I'll pay, smile, and wait until I'm the richest friend in the room.")
say("Reina", "Seven, Rich, and count it twice if it pleases you. Your Majesty, a Reservoir in Province 3, fully paid by the crown, is a promise kept. As long as the crown keeps paying its promises, I'll keep mine. If anyone at this table wants to find a cause this year, they'll find me on the other side of it.",
 "Reina: Clark is paying for my Reservoir, so I stand by him in public, and a revolt now would turn my Bowl and Jar back into crockery. I'm watching Rich: four hundred and sixty-four in a sealed reserve is just the kind of thing he'd smile his way toward.")
say("Silke", "Seven, Rich, and you can count it twice. Twenty percent of an average year is fair, so I've got no cause to call and I'm not going to invent one. Clark, that's three years at twenty and a Reservoir paid on time. It's a good ledger. Just remember the coast has paid into every year of it and hasn't had a single section of canal back.",
 "Silke: That's eighty-one and a half left, a full set, and nothing to revolt over yet. Clark's being careful, so I'll be careful too. Rich keeps thanking him in advance, and that tells me Rich is the one who's been reading the Storyteller's note on the reserve.")
say("Paddy", "Eleven, Rich, and here it is in your hand before you ask. Clark, the canal's in the ground and it's feeding Province 6 this year, so I've no cause and I won't invent one. Silke's right, though: the next section the crown digs ought to be hers.",
 "Paddy: Somebody just told Rich the reserve goes to whoever's acclaimed in Chaos, and that's four hundred and sixty-four grain, so I'm keeping my eyes on him. The man who dug my canal gets my eleven, and if a revolt comes it won't be me who called it.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Province 3 Reservoir", 80, {1:80}, key="res3", at="L12:M14", text="Public works: Clark builds the Province 3 Reservoir, two squares by three, on L12 to M14 in Reina's Province 3, at the crown's whole cost of 80, as he promised. It adds 20 to Reina's harvest from next year.", hover="Clark pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The crown may build a negotiated work alone if it pays the whole cost. The site is clear of Reina's Bowl and Jar and out of every soldier's reach this year. 610 of the works are still owed.")
act("No auctions this year: every piece of starting stock has been sold, and nobody has sold a piece back to the pile.")
march({"W1":"K13", "W2":"L16", "W3":"G12"}, take={"W2":("WB","M16")}, text="Rob marches: W2 steps onto L16 in Reina's Province 3 and takes her Wooden Bowl instead of the 5, the desert's first piece of realia, and sets it on M16 in the sand. W1 steps onto K13, raiding 5 from Reina. W3 crosses the north river onto G12 in Rich's Province 2, paying Rich a 5 toll and raiding 5 from him.")
say("Rob", "Reina, I did warn you. Four years I've been walking towards that Bowl, and you never once moved it, so I took that as an invitation. It'll be very comfortable in the desert. Your Majesty, that's three years at twenty and a Reservoir paid on time, a lovely ledger. Shame about the Warlord standing in Province 3 with the Gentry's crockery under his arm.",
 "Rob: That's the first piece in the bag: the Bowl goes to M16, W1 comes down behind it towards the Jar, and W3 pays Rich his river toll and takes it straight back off him on the way to the palaces. Silke and Paddy both said they've no cause, and I'm going to keep handing them one until they say it out loud.")
salaries({2:15}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Rich 15 as the Official, and Silke 5 for reading the I Ching as his appointee.")
act("Clark pays Silke 5 for reading the I Ching, the fee Rich set.", deltas={pid(1):-5, pid(5):5}, map_hl=[1,5])

R(6, "The population doubles", "double3")
act("Silke turns the year's I Ching card: the population doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and consumption doubles with the population: x2, 200 million. New land is open from this year.")
say("Silke", "\"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families. All provinces collect a good harvest.\" The counter goes to two hundred million, times two, so everyone read the x2 row now, and read the consumption column twice. New land is open at twenty a square, Clark, and the card lets the crown pay any part of it. My average year now comes out at minus five. You've got four hundred and sixty-four sitting in the reserve, so I'd like to hear what share of it the coast gets.",
 "Silke: That card turned \"the Emperor won't subsidise new land\" from words in the rulebook into a live cause, and it's the one Clark can't answer with a twenty percent tax. I'll buy my own square if I have to, but I want to hear him say no out loud first, in front of Paddy and Reina.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "A good harvest: forty to Province 3, forty to Paddy, twenty each to Rich and Silke, and nothing for the crown or the desert. Rent stays at five each. Silke, I know your sums went red with that card, and I'm not about to make them redder. Rob, enjoy the Bowl. I'll remember the walk you took to get it, and I'll remember how long it took.",
 "Reina: Losing the Bowl puts me two pieces from a set again, so for now my real currency is friends, and squeezing a coast that's just gone into deficit would be foolish. Rob has had his fun; next I find out what he wants for it, and whether a Customs House ever comes free.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum, and each pays her 5.")
say("Clark", "Twenty percent, four years running now. Silke, you said the coast has paid into every year and had no canal back, so both sections go in Province 5 this year, all eighty from the crown. On new land, the crown pays fifteen of every twenty on any square either of you buys on the sea, and Paddy, the desert edge too. Nobody at this table is going into debt on my watch, and nobody will be able to say I wouldn't pay for land. Rich, it's fifteen again. Rob, I've got a page with your name on it, and the Bowl has its own line.",
 "Clark: That's a canal for Silke, three-quarters of every peasant square and four squares to get the crown's own books out of the red. It costs a lot, but it shuts the debt cause and the new-land cause in one move, and it leaves Rob as the only thing anyone could hold against me. Nobody pays for land when there's a revolt on, so I want every peasant's harvest tied to this chair.")
act("Clark declares a tax of 20%: 4 each from Rich and Silke, 8 each from Reina and Paddy; Rob owes nothing on a bare harvest. The crown will dig both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 5, all 80 from the reserve. On new land, the crown will pay 15 of every 20 on any square Silke or Paddy buys this year, and will buy 4 sea squares for Province 1. At the year's end, Rich 15 as the Official and Silke 5 for reading the I Ching.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
act("Rich, Reina, Silke and Paddy each owe this year's tax, and each is asked before the Official collects: pay it, or call a revolt, or say they'd back one. Silke and Paddy are asked about new land at the same time.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Rich", "Four grain, Your Majesty, and I'll collect the rest before anyone's finished frowning. Four years at twenty, a Reservoir, a canal for the coast and fifteen for me: I couldn't have written a kinder ledger myself, and I did try once. I do have one humble petition, though. Province 2 can't buy an inch of land until the Wall links above it are built, and the card has just put me five a year in the red. Put a link or two on your page for next year and you'll have one more friend than you already have.",
 "Rich: With the population doubled, my province loses grain every year until the Wall goes up, so I need the crown spending on me, not on a revolt. I also saw the Storyteller lean over to Rob, so I'm paying quietly, keeping my hundred and twenty-odd in hand, and if a revolt does come I want to be the friendliest rich man in the room when the counting starts.")
say("Reina", "Eight, Rich, counted. Your Majesty, the Reservoir is in and I said I'd stand with you, so I do. I'll add my name to Rich's petition, though: Province 3 is shut out of new land until all four Wall links stand above it too, and a Wall keeps soldiers out as well as letting land in. Put the links on next year's page and the north will have no reason to read any other page.",
 "Reina: Clark has kept every promise he made me, so I keep mine, but a Wall-link petition shared with Rich gets me land and makes Rob's walks harder, both at once. And the Storyteller has just told Rob how a Warlord calls a revolt, so I'm watching the desert.")
say("Silke", "Four, Rich, counted. Clark, you said you'd pay the coast, and now both canal sections and fifteen of every twenty are on the page, so I'll take you at your word, all six squares of it: N26, N27, O27, P27, Q26 and Q27. That's thirty from me and ninety from the crown. Six squares at twenty a year is a hundred and twenty a year from next harvest, enough to cover an x4 card and still eat, and a peasant who isn't in the red has no cause to call. That's what you're buying.",
 "Silke: Clark took away every cause I had in my pocket, so I'm taking his money while it's on offer and leaving my set where it is until it's worth more than it's worth today. Rich and Reina are lining up for Wall links, and the next Emperor who wants the coast's backing will have to outbid ninety grain of land.")
say("Paddy", "Eight, Rich, counted out before you reach me. Clark, you said fifteen of every twenty and I'll take you at your word, same as Silke: six squares along the bottom of the coast. I'll pay my thirty today, and I'd sooner put a hundred and twenty a year into the Harvest Pile than ever sit at this table in debt.",
 "Paddy: If the x4 card comes I'll need four more squares just to stand still, so I'm buying six while the crown pays three parts in four and Rob's men are a long walk away. Clark's fed the coast two years running now, and a man who does that keeps my eight and my good word.")
taxes(.20, skip=(8,), collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={5:2}, text="Public works: Clark digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 5, at the crown's cost of 80, as he declared. Each adds 10 to Silke's harvest from next year.", hover="Clark pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. 530 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(5, ["N26","N27","O27","P27","Q26","Q27"], {5:30, 1:90}, "New land: Silke buys six squares of sea for Province 5, N26, N27, O27, P27, Q26 and Q27, paying 30; the crown pays 90, fifteen of every twenty as Clark offered. Each adds 20 to Silke's harvest from next year.")
buy_land(6, ["R25","R26","S26","T24","T25","T26"], {6:30, 1:90}, "New land: Paddy buys six squares for Province 6, R25, R26, S26, T24, T25 and T26, paying 30; the crown pays 90. Each adds 20 to Paddy's harvest from next year.")
buy_land(1, ["E23","F23","F24","F25"], {1:80}, "New land: Clark buys four squares of sea for Province 1, E23 and F23 to F25, for 80 from the crown's reserve. Each adds 20 to the crown's harvest from next year.")
march({"W1":"K14", "W2":"M17", "W3":"G13"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto K14 in Reina's Province 3, raiding 5 from her; W2 turns south-east along the desert to M17, leaving Reina's Jar where it stands; W3 steps onto G13 in Rich's Province 2, raiding 5 from him.")
say("Rob", "Reina, keep your Jar. One piece is a souvenir, two would be greed, and the desert has its standards. Your Majesty, I admire a man who spends three hundred and forty grain of the crown's money in one afternoon. Eighty left in the reserve, Rich still to pay, and the Warlord in two provinces at once. Somebody ought to write that on a page.",
 "Rob: Reina's done, so W2 turns south-east for Paddy's scroll and the coast, W1 follows, and W3 takes five off Rich on the way to the palaces. I'm not calling the revolt yet. A reserve of eighty is a cause anybody can see, and I'd rather Silke or Paddy said it first, so the desert just has to agree.")
salaries({2:15}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Rich 15 as the Official, and Silke 5 for reading the I Ching as his appointee.")
act("Clark pays Silke 5 for reading the I Ching.", deltas={pid(1):-5, pid(5):5}, map_hl=[1,5])
